WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.120
2020 was ... not a good year, to put&nbsp;
it lightly. The COVID-19 pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.520
reached practically every corner of the globe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:13.360
forcing countries to issue lockdowns. Millions&nbsp;
found themselves unemployed as businesses closed.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.480
People couldn't even escape from&nbsp;
their troubles at the movie theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:22.800
as the likes of No Time to Die, Black Widow,&nbsp;
and Fast &amp; Furious 9 all got pushed into 2021.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.200
Match.com seems to understand how uniquely&nbsp;
difficult the year was for single people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.000
In early December, in association with Ryan&nbsp;
Reynolds' production company Maximum Effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.160
the company released a commercial highlighting the&nbsp;
relatable hope that 2021 will be better than 2020.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.280
In the ad spot, Satan strikes up a new romance&nbsp;
with the human personification of 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.720
and it's a match made in Hell.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:46.960
"Call me 2020."

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.480
The commercial is enough to give&nbsp;
viewers a serious sense of déjà vu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.680
as they watch the couple exercise in an abandoned&nbsp;
gym and hoard toilet paper. But as you watch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.960
Match.com ad, you may also start to feel as though&nbsp;
you've seen the woman who plays 2020 before.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.800
As it turns out, the actress in the&nbsp;
Match.com commercial is Natalie Roy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.240
who has appeared in numerous&nbsp;
TV shows and films in the past.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.520
Roy has been acting professionally since 2005,&nbsp;
when she made her first television appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.240
in the Canadian drama series On the Run. Over&nbsp;
the years, she's popped up in various guest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:26.160
spots on a number of TV series — including&nbsp;
Blindspot on NBC and The Affair on Showtime.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:31.680
In 2018, Roy had a prominent role as Claire in&nbsp;
the short-lived dark comedy series After, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.960
follows a widower who attempts to start dating&nbsp;
once again. The show, which also stars Joshua&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:43.520
Schubart as Jeremy and Olivia Baptista as Benny,&nbsp;
was nominated for six Indie Series Awards in 2019.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.480
Roy isn't just a talent in front of the cameras&nbsp;
— she's also served as a writer, director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:54.000
and/or producer on a number of short films.&nbsp;
She released a book in 2013 called 30 Years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.480
30 Lessons, showcasing the stories,&nbsp;
dreams, and moments that make up a life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.920
as told from the perspective of&nbsp;
a 30-year-old woman trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.160
attain her goals. You can watch a promo she&nbsp;
filmed for the book on her YouTube channel.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.440
More recently, Roy appeared opposite&nbsp;
Adrian Paul, Vivica A. Fox, Sean Young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.080
and more in the thriller film A Beautiful&nbsp;
Distraction, about people who come to realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.400
that they're living in an alternate timeline,&nbsp;
completely different from the one they once did.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.840
And just a few days into 2021, Roy popped&nbsp;
up in an episode of the crime dramedy series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.320
Bull, playing a character named Olivia&nbsp;
Wright in the fifth episode of season 5.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.400
It's clear from her extensive résumé&nbsp;
that Roy is a multi-faceted talent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.160
and the Match.com 2020 commercial has introduced&nbsp;
her to a massive new audience. After "Match Made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.000
in Hell" dropped, two more ads were released:&nbsp;
"When Satan Met 2020" and "Sneak peek at 2021."

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.240
"I've dated much worse guys than him.&nbsp;
Much worse. I mean, at least he's famous."

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.360
The ads have amassed millions of views and&nbsp;
a ton of positive praise, with people really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.840
seeming to love the irreverent take on the last&nbsp;
year as well as the remastered version of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.040
country-pop song "Love Story" by Taylor Swift&nbsp;
that's featured in the "Match Made in Hell" spot.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.040
In an interview with CBC News,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.440
Roy had the chance to talk about the process&nbsp;
of bringing the commercial to life, stating,

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.200
"I do feel the scripts were so funny, and&nbsp;
the physical comedy of the difference in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:20.160
size between myself and the gentleman playing&nbsp;
the Devil was already so much great material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:23.040
that I actually feel like I&nbsp;
didn't have to do much work."

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:24.400
With so much exposure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.320
expect plenty of good things to come&nbsp;
for Natalie Roy in the years to come.

